course: dictionary
lesson: 3345
title: Exaggerate: making things bigger than they are
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To exaggerate is to make something sound bigger, better, worse or more important than it really is. People often do it when they tell a story, to make it funnier or to get attention. «I've told you a million times» is a famous exaggeration: nobody has really said it a million times.
explain: Stories and tall tales use exaggeration for fun. «The shark could smell one drop of blood from miles away» is a story that is exaggerated. Sharks do smell blood well, but the miles part is made up. So when you read, ask: is this true, or has it been made bigger?
explain: Science and maths are different. Scientists must report their results exactly, and they must not exaggerate them. A real result that is only a little better than before must be reported as just that.
explain: Some words and phrases help you spot exaggeration. «Don't exaggerate» means don't make it sound bigger than it was. A "fish story" is a phrase from the US for an exaggerated or invented claim. But be careful: when people exaggerate so often, some true words can be missed, so listen closely when someone says they cannot breathe or has real chest pain.
fact: To exaggerate means to make something sound bigger, better, worse or more important than it really is.
fact: People exaggerate in everyday life to make a story funnier or to get attention.
fact: In stories and tall tales, exaggeration is used for fun.
fact: In science and maths, you must report results exactly and not exaggerate them.
fact: «Don't exaggerate» means don't make something sound bigger than it was.
exercise: «Spinach gives no sudden muscles, but Popeye is a cartoon that exaggerates on purpose.» What does exaggerates mean here? | Makes things look bigger than they really are | *Tells the exact truth every time | Draws pictures very slowly | Eats spinach every day
exercise: Why should a scientist not exaggerate her results? | Because the results are always too small | Because bigger numbers look more fun in a book | *Because results must be reported exactly | Because science is a kind of story
exercise: «Sharks smell blood well, but the story that they can smell one drop from miles away is exaggerated.» Find and copy one word that shows the story is not true. | *exaggerated | sharks | blood | smell
test: Mum says, «Don't exaggerate, it was only a small cut.» What does she mean? | Don't cut yourself again | *Don't make it sound bigger than it was | Don't go to the hospital for a cut | Don't make a cut in the paper
test: «Celebrity feuds may be real, exaggerated or even staged for publicity.» What does exaggerated mean here? | Made to sound bigger than it really is | *Made to sound bigger than it really is, shown in a different way | Very small and quiet | Real, true and exact
exam: Why is it important to know when someone is exaggerating? | So you never need to get help | *So you can tell the true part from the made-up part | So you can make your own story bigger | So you can stop listening to everyone
